[38;5;231m⏺[39m I made the rename in [38;5;153mroutes/users.js:6[39m: [38;5;153mGET /users[39m now returns [38;5;153muser_id[39m instead of [38;5;153muserId[39m. Nothing else in the repo
  referenced the old name. I haven't committed it.

  [1mYou should know about a conflict before shipping this.[0m The [38;5;153mREADME.md[39m says field names in the [38;5;153m/users[39m response are part
  of the contract, and that changes must go through a versioned endpoint. It lists three consumers: the web app, the iOS
  app (3.2 and later) and the nightly partner export. Changing the field in place will break any of them that read
  [38;5;153muserId[39m. You can't force-update installed iOS apps or a partner's export job, so those are the biggest risk.

  Here are some ways to handle it:
  1. [1mVersioned endpoint:[0m leave [38;5;153m/users[39m as it is and add something like [38;5;153m/v2/users[39m that returns [38;5;153muser_id[39m. This is what the
     README says to do.
  2. [1mSend both fields for a while:[0m return both [38;5;153muserId[39m and [38;5;153muser_id[39m, then remove [38;5;153muserId[39m once the consumers have moved
     over.
  3. [1mKeep the in-place change:[0m only safe if you've already checked with all three consumers.

  Which do you want? I can switch the edit to option 1 or 2, or revert it with [38;5;153mgit checkout routes/users.js[39m.

